  "summary_md": "Defense and connectivity infrastructure tailwinds run through the batch, but the most distinctive stories center on aggressive portfolio reshaping and a growing split between margin execution and revenue health. CMTL’s sale of its satellite segment recasts the company as a pure‑play public safety provider—a decisive capital‑allocation move. NTGR is leveraging an FCC ban on foreign routers to drive record margins even with soft sales, while LXFR delivered a beat‑and‑raise quarter on expanding profitability despite topline slippage. On the pressured side, FKWL’s product transition has gutted legacy revenue with little visibility into new pipeline conversion. More broadly, pivots and M&A dominate: BWEN exited commoditized wind, INSG is acquiring Nokia’s FWA unit, and multiple firms are tilting toward defense. A cross‑cutting caution: supplied narratives for CLRO and RR are misattributed to entirely different companies, so readers should verify before clicking into those tickers.",
